{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "kiro-usage",
  "description": "Manual: rewrite the 'How we used Kiro' section of README.md as a table based only on the real .kiro files and tests.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Rewrite the 'How we used Kiro' section of README.md as a Markdown table with the columns: Kiro feature | What we did | Why it mattered. Rows, in this order: specs, steering, hooks, MCP, property-based testing, powers. If README.md does not exist yet, tell the developer and stop; do not create it. Replace only that section and leave the rest of the README untouched.\n\nGround every cell in evidence. Before writing, list .kiro/ (specs, steering, hooks, agents, settings) and the tests, and cite the real file names in the 'What we did' column: specs from .kiro/specs/*/ (requirements, design, tasks), steering from .kiro/steering/*.md (including the always-included rules and the manual update-kiro-usage.md), hooks from .kiro/hooks/*.kiro.hook, property-based testing from the Hypothesis tests (max_examples=200, the loaded_source_refs no-invention property) in tests/ and apps/*/tests/. For MCP and powers, check for .kiro/settings/mcp.json and for installed powers; if there is no evidence of use, write 'Not used' in the row and say why it was not needed. Never invent a feature, file, number or result we did not use; do not state test counts or coverage unless you ran the tests or read them from a fresh report. In 'Why it mattered' tie each row to the project's promise that no figure is invented and every figure traces to a source row.\n\nAfter editing, report what you checked, which rows are 'Not used', and any gap."
  }
}
